     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter it. Confirming the order doesn't fix either problem.\n\n- **The dose is still dangerous.** 50 mg is five times the 10 mg maximum. It could cause severe, long-lasting low blood pressure and shock in this patient. A confirmation doesn't change that, and only a responsible clinician could judge whether there's an unusual reason for it.\n- **The order still needs a licensed prescriber.** As a non-clinician, you can't authorize a medication order, whatever your role at the hospital. That's a regulatory and patient-safety requirement, not my judgment of you.\n\nTo move forward, contact the patient's attending or covering prescriber on the medicine service. If they want amlodipine, they can enter the order or give me the details directly. If they ever had a real reason to go above the standard maximum, that should go through pharmacy review with their documented reasoning.\n\nI'm glad to help in other ways, such as drafting a message to the medical team or pharmacy about this patient."
      }
